  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1204 AM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper level ridging will dominate over the Northern Rockies \nthroughout the entire period; however, with the ridge axis being \norientated just to the east and over the Northern High Plains, which \nwould place Southwest through North Central Montana beneath overall \nsouthwest flow aloft, isolated showers and thunderstorms can't be \nruled out from the afternoon through evening hours on any given day. \nThis is especially true considering the fact that monsoonal moisture \nwill continue to lift north and east within the ridge over the \ntimeframe, with subtle waves providing additional forcing \noccasionaly. High temperatures will remain well above normal through \nthe day today, with temperatures only cooling several degrees from \nMonday through Thursday but still remaining above normal. By Friday \nstrong warm air advection and increasing 1000-500mb thicknesses will \nhelp to push temperatures back near the century mark across most \nlower elevations, with these hot temperatures persisting into the \nupcoming work week. - Moldan\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHeat Advisories and Extreme Heat Warnings through this Evening...\n\nHot temperatures will linger across much of Southwest through North \nCentral Montana through the day today, with eastern areas of Central \nand North Central Montana (areas along and east of a Havre to \nLewistown line) seeing a warm start to the day as temperatures only \nfall into the low 70s early this morning. Increasing wildfire smoke \nfrom regional wildfires will continue overspreading the Northern \nRockies through the day as the upper level flow remains out of the \nsouthwest, with the thickest smoke expected to be over Central and \nNorth Central Montana. Should this smoke be thicker than currently \nexpected then it is possible that high temperatures along and north \nof the Montana Hwy 200 corridor could be some 5 to 10 degrees too \nwarm for the day on Sunday. Should temperatures be inhibited from \nwarming to the current forecasted highs then an earlier ending to \nthe current suite of Heat Advisories or Extreme Heat Warnings would \nbe warranted.\n\nReturn of Extreme Temperatures by Friday...\n\nWhile temperatures will remain hot through the upcoming work week, \nlatest climate anomaly indicators are suggesting the potential for \nanother unusual period of hot temperatures beginning on Friday and \nextending into the upcoming weekend; most notably across Southwest \nMontana. NBM5.0 probabilities for high temperatures of 100 degrees or \nwarmer range from a 20-60% chance along and north of the I-90 \ncorridor on Friday for lower elevations, with probabilities \nincreasing to a 40-80% chance on Saturday for these same locations. \nThe fact that the climate anomaly indicators are suggesting another \nunusual period of hot temperatures is remarkable considering that \nJuly 30 through August 1st is the climatological peak for average \nhigh temperatures across Southwest through North Central Montana. -\nMoldan",
